    'Protein design is an inverse problem: structure prediction says “give me a sequence and I will tell you the shape”, design says “give me a shape and a function and I will write a sequence”. How that problem is solved has changed three times in twenty years: first physics-based scoring plus search (Rosetta, and Top7 in 2003), then deep learning taking over both the judging (AlphaFold-class models became the referee for designs) and the writing (ProteinMPNN writes sequences, RFdiffusion generates backbones), and in the last two years general-purpose models pushing in as well — in 2025 OpenAI redesigned two reprogramming factors with a small specialised model, and in November 2026 it reported that GPT-6-class models can design binders without a specialised design model in the loop. This section lays the results out: every one carries a level of evidence (validated in the wet lab / computational only / a company statement only) and a note on how open its weights are. Each chapter comes with a demo computed on the spot, so that the arithmetic behind the numbers everybody quotes — hit rates, K_D, k_cat/K_M, recovery — is explicit. The last chapter is about biosecurity: the same toolkit has to be able to design medicines while leaving screening able to recognise what should not be synthesised.',
